//~ compile_fail(backend)
//~ error[KORU030]: Resource 'node' obligation

// NEGATIVE (must FAIL to compile): walk to a fid and never give it back.
//
// This is the middle level of the nesting, and it is worth its own file because
// of what it is NOT. `unikraft/alloc` deliberately does not claim "forgetting to
// free is a compile error", and its README says why: a dropped `<live!>` block
// has exactly one unattended terminal disposer, so the compiler INSERTS the
// `free` and reports nothing. Correct behaviour; not a catch.
//
// `unikraft/ninep` CAN make the claim at every level, and it is the same
// mechanism reaching the opposite outcome rather than a different mechanism.
// `<walked>`'s only consumers are `file.open` and `file.close`, and NEITHER is a
// disposal — `file.open` mints `<opened!>` and `file.close` mints `<rooted!>` on
// the parent. There is no terminal for auto-discharge to elect anywhere in this
// module, at any of the three levels, because every destructor here hands back
// a live parent obligation. The nesting is what makes leak-detection total.
//
// WHAT THE C DOES INSTEAD: exactly what
// `negative_disconnect_with_file_open.kz` describes, one level down. The fid
// stays on `dev->_fid_mgmt.fid_active_list`, the server keeps it open, and the
// only thing standing between that and a disconnect is 9pdev.c:118 — an
// assertion that is `do {} while(0)` in every shipped image.
//
// The three-way split for this module, stated exactly:
//
//   * forget to CLOSE a file        -> compile ERROR (this file)
//   * forget to RELEASE a reply     -> compile ERROR (negative_reply_leaked.kz)
//   * forget to USE a connection    -> compile ERROR
//                                      (negative_disconnect_without_session.kz)
//
// There is no fourth case where something is silently inserted, and that is a
// property of the composition rule, not a coincidence.
//
// EXPECTED: error[KORU030]: Resource 'node' obligation <walked!> was not
//           discharged. Call: file.close

// The `unikraft` namespace is declared here, not inherited: koruc's built-in
// alias probes the sibling koru-libs CHECKOUT, so in a worktree this file would
// otherwise compile against main's lifts instead of the ones beside it.
~std/compiler:paths {
    unikraft: {{ ENTRY }}/../..
}

~import unikraft/ninep
~import std/io

~unikraft/ninep:connect(tag: "korufs")
| ok mount |> unikraft/ninep:version(dev: mount, requested: "9P2000.L")
    | negotiated v |> unikraft/ninep:reply.release(v.reply)
        | ok rel |> unikraft/ninep:attach(rel.dev, uname: "korunaut", aname: "")
            | ok tree |> unikraft/ninep:walk(root: tree, name: "koru.txt")
                | ok node |> std/io:print.ln("walked, and nothing ever gives the fid back")
                | refused e |> std/io:print.ln("walk: {{ e.reason:s }}")
            | refused e |> std/io:print.ln("attach: {{ e.reason:s }}")
    | refused e |> std/io:print.ln("version: {{ e.reason:s }}")
| absent why |> std/io:print.ln("connect: {{ why:s }}")
| refused e |> std/io:print.ln("connect: {{ e.reason:s }}")
